{
  "slug": "passe-compose",
  "number": 10,
  "part": 3,
  "title": "The passé composé",
  "tagline": "Talking about what happened: avoir or être, past participles and agreement.",
  "goal": "Say what you did yesterday or at the weekend, choose between avoir and être and make the past participle agree.",
  "minutes": 12,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "formula",
      "title": "The formula",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "avoir or être in the present + past participle",
          "caption": "*J'{ai mangé}. · Nous {avons regardé} un film.*"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "It's built like «I have eaten», but it's used for both «I ate» and «I have eaten». You already know *avoir* and *être* [[4]]; you just need the participle."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "regulares",
      "title": "Regular participles",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Infinitive",
            "Participle",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**-er → -é**",
              "*parler → parl{é}*",
              "*J'ai parlé avec Marie.*"
            ],
            [
              "**-ir → -i**",
              "*finir → fin{i}*",
              "*Tu as fini ton rapport ?*"
            ],
            [
              "**-re → -u**",
              "*vendre → vend{u}*",
              "*Il a vendu sa voiture.*"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "irregulares",
      "title": "Irregular participles",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Verb",
            "Participle",
            "Verb",
            "Participle"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*être*",
              "*été*",
              "*voir* (to see)",
              "*vu*"
            ],
            [
              "*avoir*",
              "*eu*",
              "*lire* (to read)",
              "*lu*"
            ],
            [
              "*faire*",
              "*fait*",
              "*boire* (to drink)",
              "*bu*"
            ],
            [
              "*prendre* (to take)",
              "*pris*",
              "*vouloir*",
              "*voulu*"
            ],
            [
              "*écrire* (to write)",
              "*écrit*",
              "*pouvoir*",
              "*pu*"
            ],
            [
              "*mettre* (to put)",
              "*mis*",
              "*dire* (to say)",
              "*dit*"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "etre",
      "title": "Verbs that take être",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Most verbs use *avoir*. A small group uses **être**: almost all of them describe **movement or a change of state**."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Verbs",
            "Participles"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*aller / venir*",
              "*allé / venu*"
            ],
            [
              "*arriver / partir*",
              "*arrivé / parti*"
            ],
            [
              "*entrer / sortir*",
              "*entré / sorti*"
            ],
            [
              "*monter / descendre*",
              "*monté / descendu*"
            ],
            [
              "*tomber*",
              "*tombé*"
            ],
            [
              "*rester*",
              "*resté*"
            ],
            [
              "*naître / mourir*",
              "*né / mort*"
            ],
            [
              "*devenir*",
              "*devenu*"
            ],
            [
              "*rentrer / retourner / revenir*",
              "*rentré / retourné / revenu*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "text": "Picture a house: you arrive and leave, go in and out, go up and down the stairs, fall, stay, come back. And **every reflexive verb** takes *être* too [[17]]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "concordancia",
      "title": "Agreement with être",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "With *être*, the participle behaves like an adjective and **agrees with the subject**:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Subject",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "masculine singular",
              "*Il est allé à Paris.*"
            ],
            [
              "feminine singular",
              "*Elle est allé{e} à Paris.*"
            ],
            [
              "masculine plural",
              "*Ils sont allé{s} à Paris.*"
            ],
            [
              "feminine plural",
              "*Elles sont allé{es} à Paris.*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "*allé, allée, allés, allées* sound exactly the same. The difference only shows in writing."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "text": "With *avoir*, the participle doesn't agree with the subject: *Elle a mangé*, no e."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "negacion",
      "title": "Negation and time words",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "*ne… pas* goes around the **auxiliary**:"
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "Je n'ai pas mangé.",
              "tr": "I haven't eaten. / I didn't eat."
            },
            {
              "fr": "Elle n'est pas venue.",
              "tr": "She didn't come."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "**Talking about the past:** *hier* (yesterday) · *avant-hier* (the day before yesterday) · *ce matin* (this morning) · *le week-end dernier* (last weekend) · *la semaine dernière* (last week) · *l'année dernière* (last year) · *il y a deux jours* (two days ago)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To describe the past (what things were like, what was happening) French uses the *imparfait* [[18]]."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
